<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James II of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1267–1327</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as James the Just</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James II of Aragon, called the Just, was King of Aragon from 1291 and earlier King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. In 1297 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a> granted him the new Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, and his son Alfonso conquered the Pisan parts of Sardinia in 1323 and 1324. His reign extended the Crown of Aragon's power across the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-ii-of-aragon.jpg" alt="James II of Aragon" width="320" height="505" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonym - vers 1315-1325 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jacques_2_d%27aragon.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
